A||||16||Cozmo||Alternator belt squeal?||||5||
Z||000000||Cozmo||07-22-2001||09:23 AM||||On my 2M6 Targa the alternator squeals on startup sometimes.  Pretty much everything is new, and as well maintained as I can keep it.<BR>It only does it for about 2-3 seconds after the car has sat for a few days.  Belt play is about 1/2", I've even tried belt dressing with no improvement.<BR>This is a REAL minor problem, but it's irritating... I get into my toy after a few days to go have fun and she squeals at me <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Any suggestions on proper belt protocol??||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||07-22-2001||10:29 AM||||Just a little loose still. If it's a new belt and recently installed and it had 1/2" play to begin with, it has stretched already which is typical of most belts.<BR>I wish they had put a tensioner on those engines, I spend a lot of time tightening belts!||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-22-2001||12:15 PM||||Replace the belt. Since you've let it go, it's worn now... (It likely is slipping all the time but you probably can't here it.) They are cheap so get anohter one. Save this one for an emergency spare. (Also, Many "Belt Dressing" products can actually damage the belt. I would not trust this one long term.)<P>Install the new belt then run it awhile. 5-10 minutes. Then let it cool and retension the belt. This should get you a better result.<P>Also, clean the pulleys with brake parts cleaner. If there is any grease on them it will make the belt slip and glaze. You also want to remove any residue from the belt dressing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cozmo||07-22-2001||03:08 PM||||Thanks!<BR>I'll pick up a new belt and install it as suggested. What is a good quality brand to use? (thank goodness I'm not asking about spark plugs <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||07-22-2001||04:04 PM||||I really like Gates belts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RoadRocket||07-22-2001||05:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>I really like Gates belts.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Awe man! Did Bill Gates invent those too!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Sorry, couldn't resist.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||07-23-2001||08:51 PM||||Gates if you can find them otherwise Dayco or NAPA and watch how you tighten them. Over is as bad as loose. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||07-24-2001||10:50 AM||||I found something that works just right for tensioning the alt belt on a V6. I was gonna use the crow bar but I couldnt find it, so I grabbed the handle off my hydralic floor jack.<P>  you can wedge it from the top side between the alt housing and the block - almost like it was made for it.<P>  gotta be careful you dont pinch the cable thats down there, and make sure you are on the alt body, not the tin cover on its backside.<P>  I think if I coulda found it a crow bar would be perfect too. only problem is its a two man job, one to hold the tension while the other tightens it up through the dust flap in the wheel well.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiero_24||07-24-2001||11:48 AM||||You may end up like me and have it snap after neglecting it.  I had to drive home 1 mile without a belt, luckily it was in the country and I had a lot of hills to go down.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||LZeitgeist||07-24-2001||01:13 PM||||Yeah, I can't believe they didn't put *ANY* sort of tensioner on the V6's!!! Argh!! I *hate* belt squeal... <P>After a really good wash and wax, I slide into the driver's seat with my sunglasses on, feeling like Magnum,P.I. getting into his Ferrari, and I turn the key... and rabid chipmunks start a freaking mosh pit in my engine compartment. <P>Oh, yeah... *that*'s sexy...<P>I rev the engine a couple times to try to get it to quit, and then I slink off down the street like I'm in a ragged-out '63 VW Bug with a fender draggin on the pavement.<P>Damn Fiero 'quirks'... :-P||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||07-24-2001||01:35 PM||||I always associate that belt squeel with an 80 year old lady trying to turn a '79 caddy into a parking spot, with the power steering hitting the limit, and the belt screaming for mercy.<P>  but to no avail - she cant hear it anyway, and it takes her 3 minutes to complete the turn.<P>  - yeah! it is a cool sexy sound, kinda like doing a bleach burn with straight pipes on your V8 - chicks dig it :c)||||reg||10
Z||000011||bHooper||07-24-2001||05:24 PM||||is it auto, or straight drive?  on my 5 spd, it is relatively easy.. just take a bar, or jack handle and wedge it between the block an dthe altenator, and tighten. <P>on the auto.. well, i haven't figured that one out yet.  i've had that car 2 months and the belt has squeeked th whole time.  i will probably wind up taking it to a shop to get fixed!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||07-24-2001||05:38 PM||||whats the difference - the alt is on the other end of the motor from the tranny - its in the same location, isnt it?!||||reg||1
Z||000013||My7Fieros||07-25-2001||02:41 AM||||Like Ogre said...<B><FONT COLOR="blue"><FONT SIZE="3">DAYCO BRAND BELT</B></FONT></FONT>...that'll remedy your prob...believe me, I know. Been through it on everyone of my Fieros lol.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000014||bHooper||07-25-2001||08:41 AM||||Ken, ishould'a known that there were people out there (like i used to be) who thought all the tranny's were the same... the automatic tranny wrapps around the block a bit.  or think of it as having a shorter (or maybe equal) length half shafts, instead of one long one as the manuals do.<P>therefore, youi have tranny underneath the altenator, and it is a PITA to get at. <P><BR>as for belt brands, i have tried: NAPA, Advance, Autozone, and Carquest.  my favorite is Carquest (they waranty their belts!)<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||12
Z||000015||87GTZ34||07-27-2001||05:28 PM||||Hoop,  For the automatic, I take the wheel off and go from underneath.  There is a spot to put a prybar between the alt and the bracket and hold 'it' w/ one hand while using the other to tighen the slide bolt. As Ogre mentioned, don't pull like a gorilla on the prybar or the belt will stretch beyond its limit and it will always squeel like pig.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000016||kslish||07-28-2001||12:06 AM||||I'm a big fan of Goodyear Gatorback belts myself.  They have yet to squeak once on my GT.  They are a couple of bucks more, but worth it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
